      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We describe 3 patients with painful intraarticular knee effusions composed of a viscous milky white suspension of monosodium urate crystals, in the absence of any cellular component. Two patients presented with acute bilateral knee pain. One patient presented with unilateral knee pain of gradual onset. All 3 patients had a history of ethanol abuse. Two patients had a history of gout. Two patients had chronic renal insufficiency, hypertension, and congestive heart failure. One patient had alcoholic cirrhosis. Two patients' pain responded to colchicine. One patient's discomfort was relieved only by repeated arthrocentesis. We conclude that intraarticular free urate can cause painful joints in the absence of an apparent inflammatory response."},
